1
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:43,880
Don't think too much, Bran.

2
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,230
Relax your bow arm.

3
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:58,220
And which one of you was a marksman at 10?

4
00:02:58,780 --> 00:02:59,810
Father?

5
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,270
You can't kill it, you know.

6
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:11,780
Why not?

7
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:16,400
Because the raven is you.

8
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:28,160
Hodor.

9
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:29,980
It's all right, Hodor.

10
00:03:30,970 --> 00:03:31,970
Hodor.

11
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:39,580
Were you inside the wolf again, little lord?

12
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:42,950
No, it was the three-eyed raven.

13
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:44,200
He's back.

14
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:48,280
I tried to kill it, but I couldn't.

15
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:50,650
- There was a boy. - I don't want to hear about it.

16
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:53,330
- But you asked. - We've got plenty of worries.

17
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:55,460
We don't need to pour black magic on top of them.

18
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,060
I didn't ask for black magic dreams.

19
00:03:59,370 --> 00:04:01,160
I know you didn't, little lord.

20
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:04,280
We need to move.

21
00:04:05,090 --> 00:04:06,670
We don't know who might be after us.

22
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:08,750
No one even knows we're alive.

23
00:04:09,330 --> 00:04:10,750
And who told you that?

24
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:13,020
The three-eyed raven tell you?

25
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,530
- No. - Some good he is, then.

26
00:04:18,370 --> 00:04:20,880
I don't know what other people know and what they don't.

27
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:23,850
I only know the Wall is a long way off.

28
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:35,270
My mother always told me

29
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:37,720
you Westerosi were a grim lot.

30
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:41,930
Grim, bearded,

31
00:04:41,930 --> 00:04:43,750
stinking barbarians

32
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,910
that would row across the Narrow Sea and steal us from our beds.

33
00:04:48,970 --> 00:04:50,920
Did you ever think you'd marry one?

34
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:55,310
I never thought I'd marry anyone at all.

35
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:56,810
Never?

36
00:04:58,340 --> 00:05:01,560
Not until I met the king of the grim,

37
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:03,740
bearded, stinking barbarians.

38
00:05:10,350 --> 00:05:12,030
Pardon me, Your Grace.

39
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,340
- My queen. - Lord Bolton.

40
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,990
Let me guess which one is the good news.

41
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:25,920
Word from Riverrun and Winterfell.

42
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:33,150
I hadn't seen him in years.

43
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:36,950
I don't even know how many.

44
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,930
We'll travel to the funeral together.

45
00:05:41,830 --> 00:05:44,660
Lord Bolton will garrison here until we return.

46
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:49,340
Will I be wearing manacles when I lay my father to rest?

47
00:05:55,470 --> 00:05:56,870
Something else?

48
00:05:58,910 --> 00:06:01,000
By the time Bolton's bastard got to Winterfell,

49
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:02,560
the ironborn were gone.

50
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:07,710
They massacred everyone and put the castle to the torch.

51
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:13,100
And Bran and Rickon have not been found.

52
00:06:17,310 --> 00:06:18,870
They may have escaped.

53
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:22,820
Or Theon may have taken them back to the Iron Islands as hostages.

54
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:24,730
Have you received any demands?

55
00:06:26,370 --> 00:06:27,180
No.

56
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:29,800
Have you heard anything from Theon at all?

57
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:53,230
Where am I?

58
00:07:00,190 --> 00:07:01,640
Who are you?

59
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:09,350
What do you want?

60
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:11,120
I want to do this.

61
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:18,670
Do you know how long it's going to take us

62
00:07:18,670 --> 00:07:21,530
to get to King's Landing walking through fields and forests?

63
00:07:21,530 --> 00:07:22,580
Yes.

64
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,080
So how shall we pass the time?

65
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:27,790
By putting one foot in front of the other.

66
00:07:27,790 --> 00:07:30,190
It's going to be a very dull walk.

67
00:07:30,190 --> 00:07:32,000
I'm here to take you to King's Landing

68
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,440
and bring back Lady Stark's daughters in exchange.

69
00:07:34,730 --> 00:07:36,440
Dull is fine.

70
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:39,270
You know, it doesn't matter how loyal a servant you are,

71
00:07:39,570 --> 00:07:42,330
no one enjoys the company of a humorless mute.

72
00:07:42,330 --> 00:07:43,830
Trust me on this.

73
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:45,920
People have been serving me since I was born.

74
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:49,460
You think Lady Stark is going to want a giant towheaded plank

75
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:51,340
following her around for the rest of her life?

76
00:07:52,330 --> 00:07:53,410
A week's journey with you

77
00:07:53,410 --> 00:07:55,290
and she'll order you to fall on your sword.

78
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:59,120
If Lady Stark is unhappy with any aspect of my service,

79
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,750
I'm sure she'll let me know.

80
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:02,560
She's an honest woman.

81
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:04,380
All the good it's done her.

82
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:13,190
How did you come into Lady Stark's service?

83
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:14,630
There's something we can talk about.

84
00:08:14,630 --> 00:08:16,340
Not your concern, Kingslayer.

85
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:17,720
It had to be recently.

86
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:19,740
You weren't with her at Winterfell.

87
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:21,850
How would you know?

88
00:08:22,090 --> 00:08:24,510
Because I visited Winterfell.

89
00:08:24,650 --> 00:08:28,040
I would have noticed your dour head smacking into the archways.

90
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:30,060
Move.

91
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:37,770
- Were you pledged to Stannis? - Gods, no.

92
00:08:38,170 --> 00:08:39,620
Ah, Renly.

93
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:41,150
Really?

94
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:43,080
He wasn't fit to rule over anything

95
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:44,990
more important than a 12-course meal.

96
00:08:44,990 --> 00:08:46,800
- Shut your mouth. - Why?

97
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:49,330
I lived with him at court since he was a boy, don't forget.

98
00:08:49,970 --> 00:08:52,030
Could hardly escape the little tulip.

99
00:08:52,050 --> 00:08:55,170
Skipping down the corridors in his embroidered silks.

100
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:56,790
I knew him far better than you.

101
00:08:56,790 --> 00:08:58,620
I knew him as well as anyone.

102
00:08:58,830 --> 00:09:00,430
As a member of his Kingsguard,

103
00:09:00,430 --> 00:09:02,130
he trusted me with everything.

104
00:09:02,530 --> 00:09:03,920
He would have been a wonderful king.

105
00:09:03,930 --> 00:09:05,260
Sounds like you quite fancied him.

106
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:07,600
I did not fancy him.

107
00:09:07,910 --> 00:09:09,420
Oh, gods, you did.

108
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:11,770
Did you ever tell him?

109
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,290
Of course not.

110
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:17,330
You weren't Renly's type, I'm afraid.

111
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:21,430
He preferred curly-haired little girls like Loras Tyrell.

112
00:09:21,570 --> 00:09:23,160
You're far too much man for him.

113
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:25,030
I'm not interested in foul rumors.

114
00:09:25,030 --> 00:09:26,550
Unless they're about me.

115
00:09:27,270 --> 00:09:28,850
It's all true about Renly.

116
00:09:29,410 --> 00:09:32,300
His proclivities were the worst kept secret at court.

117
00:09:32,330 --> 00:09:34,480
It's a shame the throne isn't made out of cocks.

118
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:37,360
- They'd have never got him off it. - Shut your mouth!

119
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:41,040
I don't blame him.

120
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:42,810
And I don't blame you, either.

121
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:46,460
We don't get to choose who we love.

122
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:00,280
- Where are you headed, then? - South.

123
00:10:00,430 --> 00:10:02,170
- You? - Riverrun.

124
00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:05,200
Staying off the kingsroad, are you?

125
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:07,670
They get you no matter where you go.

126
00:10:07,670 --> 00:10:10,070
- You can't win. - No, you really can't.

127
00:10:10,070 --> 00:10:11,800
Looks like you're safe enough, though.

128
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:13,800
Meaning no offense, my lady, but...

129
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:15,940
I wouldn't tangle with you.

130
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:19,930
- Seven blessings to you. - And you.

131
00:10:27,310 --> 00:10:28,780
He knows who I am.

132
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,550
- He doesn't. - Maybe you're right.

133
00:10:30,550 --> 00:10:33,280
But what if you're not? What if he tells someone?

134
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:37,460
We're not doing it.

135
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:40,080
He's an innocent man.

136
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:42,890
More innocent than Lady Stark's daughters?

137
00:10:54,700 --> 00:10:56,260
Make it tighter here.

138
00:10:58,190 --> 00:10:59,500
That's better.

139
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:02,450
It makes you look very strong.

140
00:11:02,450 --> 00:11:04,070
No flowers.

141
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:06,440
I said no flowers.

142
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,230
All these are wrong. Bring others.

143
00:11:10,230 --> 00:11:11,590
Yes, Your Grace.

144
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:15,230
Bloody flowers.

145
00:11:15,410 --> 00:11:16,770
On the king.

146
00:11:17,480 --> 00:11:19,270
Looks like a Tyrell rose.

147
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,520
Give it to Margaery for her wedding gown.

148
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:24,750
Should be more than enough fabric.

149
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:31,780
Tell me what you think of her.

150
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:33,310
Who?

151
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:35,200
Margaery.

152
00:11:35,370 --> 00:11:36,920
She's an ideal match.

153
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:40,810
With the Tyrells beside us, we'll crush the Northerners.

154
00:11:41,510 --> 00:11:44,650
Hang their lords, burn their strongholds,

155
00:11:44,650 --> 00:11:46,250
sow their fields with salt,

156
00:11:46,250 --> 00:11:49,280
and no one will think of rebelling for another century.

157
00:11:51,130 --> 00:11:53,620
It is a good match, but what do you think of her?

158
00:11:57,790 --> 00:12:00,350
She's beautiful and intelligent.

159
00:12:00,790 --> 00:12:02,130
Yes, she is.

160
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:05,880
Her concern with the well-being of the common people is interesting.

161
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:07,830
Not to me.

162
00:12:10,050 --> 00:12:12,540
I only meant that to go out of your way to endanger yourself...

163
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:16,710
This is becoming one of the most boring conversations I've ever had.

164
00:12:20,630 --> 00:12:23,830
Margaery Tyrell dotes on filthy urchins for a reason.

165
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:26,870
She dresses like a harlot for a reason.

166
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:29,910
She married a traitor and known degenerate

167
00:12:29,910 --> 00:12:31,560
like Renly Baratheon for a reason.

168
00:12:31,570 --> 00:12:34,560
She married Renly Baratheon because she was told to.

169
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:37,310
That's what intelligent women do--

170
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:39,500
what they're told.

171
00:12:42,370 --> 00:12:46,610
- You need to ask yourself-- - I don't need to do anything.

172
00:12:57,740 --> 00:12:58,760
Better.

173
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:02,480
Much better.

174
00:13:03,650 --> 00:13:05,200
I still don't believe it.

175
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:07,020
He didn't. He didn't want anything.

176
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:10,650
He just takes an interest because he loved my mother.

177
00:13:12,460 --> 00:13:15,550
Men only want one thing from a pretty girl.

178
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:20,050
Littlefinger's not in love with me.

179
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:25,010
Love is not the thing he wants.

180
00:13:27,310 --> 00:13:29,020
He's too old.

181
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:30,830
They never see it that way.

182
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:37,960
He didn't ask you to do something for him?

183
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:40,010
Spy on someone or...?

184
00:13:40,270 --> 00:13:41,270
No.

185
00:13:47,910 --> 00:13:50,190
If he does ask you for anything

186
00:13:50,350 --> 00:13:52,020
or try anything

187
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:55,500
or touch you, I want you to tell me.

188
00:13:56,840 --> 00:13:58,480
Why? What will you do?

189
00:13:59,250 --> 00:14:01,040
I will make him stop.

190
00:14:03,110 --> 00:14:05,200
Ser Loras Tyrell, my lady.

191
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:07,580
Send him in.

192
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:18,230
Lady Sansa.

193
00:14:18,290 --> 00:14:19,590
Ser Loras.

194
00:14:19,590 --> 00:14:21,880
My sister Margaery invites you to take the air

195
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:24,470
with her and our grandmother the Lady Olenna in the gardens.

196
00:14:25,220 --> 00:14:26,830
May I escort you to them?

197
00:14:34,390 --> 00:14:37,060
I believe you grow more beautiful every day, Lady Sansa.

198
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:39,180
You are too kind, Ser Loras.

199
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:45,010
You probably don't remember the first time we met.

200
00:14:46,630 --> 00:14:49,460
At the Hand's tourney, you gave me your favor.

201
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:53,260
A rose, a red rose.

202
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:55,400
Of course I did.

203
00:14:58,570 --> 00:14:59,860
It's more your color.

204
00:14:59,860 --> 00:15:02,360
- You take it. - Thank you.

205
00:15:02,770 --> 00:15:04,320
You're such a dear.

206
00:15:04,490 --> 00:15:06,120
I'll take my leave.

207
00:15:06,890 --> 00:15:08,150
Lady Sansa.

208
00:15:08,410 --> 00:15:09,980
Thank you, Ser Loras.

209
00:15:11,130 --> 00:15:12,170
Come.

210
00:15:18,710 --> 00:15:23,030
Lady Sansa, it is my honor to present my grandmother...

211
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:26,700
the Lady Olenna of House Tyrell.

212
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:28,540
Kiss me, child.

213
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:30,240
It's so good of you

214
00:15:30,270 --> 00:15:32,950
to visit me and my foolish flock of hens.

215
00:15:33,050 --> 00:15:35,080
We're very sorry for your losses.

216
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:37,160
And I was sorry when I heard of

217
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:39,190
Lord Renly's death, Lady Margaery.

218
00:15:39,220 --> 00:15:41,260
- He was very gallant. - Gallant, yes.

219
00:15:41,260 --> 00:15:43,200
And charming and very clean.

220
00:15:43,290 --> 00:15:45,090
He knew how to dress and smile

221
00:15:45,090 --> 00:15:48,160
and somehow this gave him the notion he was fit to be king.

222
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:50,390
Renly was brave and gentle, Grandmother.

223
00:15:50,390 --> 00:15:52,190
Father liked him and so did Loras.

224
00:15:52,190 --> 00:15:53,630
Loras is young and very good

225
00:15:53,630 --> 00:15:55,670
at knocking men off horses with a stick.

226
00:15:55,670 --> 00:15:57,590
That does not make him wise.

227
00:15:57,880 --> 00:16:00,200
As to your fathead father...

228
00:16:00,210 --> 00:16:02,730
Grandmother! What will Sansa think of us?

229
00:16:02,730 --> 00:16:04,700
She might think we have some wits about us.

230
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:05,990
One of us, at any rate.

231
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:09,390
It was treason. I warned them.

232
00:16:09,790 --> 00:16:12,890
Robert has two sons and Renly has an older brother.

233
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:16,710
How could he possibly have any claim to that ugly iron chair?

234
00:16:16,970 --> 00:16:20,160
We should have stayed well out of all this if you ask me.

235
00:16:20,380 --> 00:16:22,280
But once the cow's been milked,

236
00:16:22,280 --> 00:16:24,750
there's no squirting the cream back up her udders.

237
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:26,780
So here we are to see things through.

238
00:16:27,010 --> 00:16:28,840
What do you say to that, Sansa?

239
00:16:30,050 --> 00:16:32,140
Shall we have some lemon cakes?

240
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:34,390
Lemon cake's my favorite.

241
00:16:34,390 --> 00:16:36,120
So we've been told.

242
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:39,920
Are you going to bring the food or do you mean to starve us to death?

243
00:16:40,690 --> 00:16:42,090
Here, Sansa,

244
00:16:42,390 --> 00:16:43,950
come sit with me.

245
00:16:45,380 --> 00:16:48,210
I'm much less boring than these others.

246
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:51,810
Do you know my son?

247
00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:55,070
The Lord of Highgarden?

248
00:16:55,070 --> 00:16:56,630
I haven't had the pleasure.

249
00:16:57,230 --> 00:16:59,340
No great pleasure, believe me.

250
00:16:59,370 --> 00:17:00,790
A ponderous oaf.

251
00:17:00,790 --> 00:17:02,560
His father was an oaf as well.

252
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:05,010
My husband, the late Lord Luthor.

253
00:17:05,330 --> 00:17:08,460
He managed to ride off a cliff whilst hawking.

254
00:17:08,840 --> 00:17:10,830
They say he was looking up at the sky

255
00:17:10,830 --> 00:17:13,830
and paying no mind to where his horse was taking him.

256
00:17:14,330 --> 00:17:16,320
And now my son is doing the same,

257
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:19,370
only this time he's riding a lion instead of a horse.

258
00:17:20,130 --> 00:17:21,500
Now...

259
00:17:22,570 --> 00:17:24,970
I want you to tell me the truth

260
00:17:24,970 --> 00:17:28,360
about this royal boy, this Joffrey.

261
00:17:29,210 --> 00:17:31,270
I... I...

262
00:17:31,270 --> 00:17:32,670
You, you.

263
00:17:32,680 --> 00:17:34,260
Who else would know better?

264
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:37,110
We've heard some troubling tales.

265
00:17:37,370 --> 00:17:39,100
Is there any truth to them?

266
00:17:39,820 --> 00:17:41,790
Has this boy mistreated you?

267
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:44,910
Has he ripped out your tongue?

268
00:17:44,910 --> 00:17:47,170
Joff-- King Joffrey,

269
00:17:47,170 --> 00:17:51,680
he-- His Grace is very fair and handsome

270
00:17:51,680 --> 00:17:53,500
and as brave as a lion.

271
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:56,410
Yes, all Lannisters are lions.

272
00:17:56,480 --> 00:18:00,020
And when a Tyrell farts, it smells like a rose.

273
00:18:00,360 --> 00:18:02,620
But how kind is he? How clever?

274
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:04,870
Has he a good heart, a gentle hand?

275
00:18:04,870 --> 00:18:06,310
I'm to be his wife.

276
00:18:06,310 --> 00:18:08,440
I only want to know what that means.

277
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:18,070
Bring me some cheese.

278
00:18:18,070 --> 00:18:20,310
The cheese will be served after the cakes, my lady.

279
00:18:20,310 --> 00:18:22,660
The cheese will be served when I want it served.

280
00:18:22,660 --> 00:18:24,470
And I want it served now.

281
00:18:28,340 --> 00:18:30,070
Are you frightened, child?

282
00:18:30,540 --> 00:18:32,270
No need for that.

283
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:34,350
We're only women here.

284
00:18:34,530 --> 00:18:37,740
Tell us the truth. No harm will come to you.

285
00:18:38,910 --> 00:18:41,010
My father always told the truth.

286
00:18:41,070 --> 00:18:43,930
Yes, he had that reputation.

287
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,330
And they named him traitor and took his head.

288
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:47,590
Joffrey.

289
00:18:49,280 --> 00:18:50,890
Joffrey did that.

290
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:54,660
He promised he would be merciful and he cut my father's head off.

291
00:18:54,660 --> 00:18:56,610
And he said that was mercy.

292
00:18:56,930 --> 00:19:00,180
Then he took me up on the walls and made me look at it.

293
00:19:04,410 --> 00:19:05,550
Go on.

294
00:19:07,390 --> 00:19:10,540
I-- I can't. I never meant--

295
00:19:10,750 --> 00:19:12,660
my father was a traitor.

296
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:15,330
My brother as well. I have traitor's blood.

297
00:19:15,570 --> 00:19:17,030
Please don't make me say anymore.

298
00:19:17,030 --> 00:19:18,370
She's terrified, Grandmother.

299
00:19:18,370 --> 00:19:21,650
- Just look at her. - Speak freely, child.

300
00:19:22,050 --> 00:19:25,500
We would never betray your confidence, I swear it.

301
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:29,630
He's a monster.

302
00:19:34,250 --> 00:19:35,450
That's a pity.

303
00:19:35,450 --> 00:19:37,260
Please, don't stop the wedding.

304
00:19:37,450 --> 00:19:40,500
Have no fear. The Lord Oaf of Highgarden

305
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:43,400
is determined that Margaery shall be queen.

306
00:19:43,750 --> 00:19:46,070
Even so, we thank you for the truth.

307
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:49,790
Ah, here comes my cheese.

308
00:20:05,750 --> 00:20:07,060
We're at war.

309
00:20:07,060 --> 00:20:09,160
This march is a distraction.

310
00:20:09,610 --> 00:20:12,800
My grandfather's funeral is not a distraction.

311
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:15,740
- Are we riding to battle at Riverrun? - No.

312
00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:17,300
Then it's a distraction.

313
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:21,010
My Uncle Edmure has his forces garrisoned there.

314
00:20:21,010 --> 00:20:22,590
We need his men.

315
00:20:22,920 --> 00:20:25,060
Unless he's been breeding them,

316
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:27,680
he don't have enough to make a difference.

317
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:29,910
Have you lost faith in our cause?

318
00:20:30,270 --> 00:20:33,950
If it's revenge, I still got faith in it.

319
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:35,190
If you no longer believe--

320
00:20:35,190 --> 00:20:37,860
I can believe till it snows in Dorne.

321
00:20:38,130 --> 00:20:41,370
Don't change the fact that we've got half the men.

322
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:45,290
You don't think we can win?

323
00:20:46,350 --> 00:20:48,380
May I speak my mind, Your Grace?

324
00:20:48,390 --> 00:20:51,580
Have you not been speaking your mind, Lord Karstark?

325
00:20:52,130 --> 00:20:54,450
I think you lost this war

326
00:20:55,020 --> 00:20:57,270
the day you married her.

327
00:21:10,510 --> 00:21:11,340
Your Grace.

328
00:21:11,350 --> 00:21:12,980
You're afraid of her.

329
00:21:13,970 --> 00:21:16,690
- And she knows it. - I'm not afraid of her.

330
00:21:27,090 --> 00:21:29,160
May I help you, Lady Stark?

331
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:30,190
No.

332
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:33,270
I'm sorry, I shouldn't have--

333
00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:35,020
You can't help because a mother

334
00:21:35,020 --> 00:21:37,710
makes one for her children to protect them.

335
00:21:39,860 --> 00:21:41,850
Only a mother can make them.

336
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:50,340
You've made them before?

337
00:21:52,570 --> 00:21:53,570
Twice.

338
00:21:55,720 --> 00:21:57,100
Did they work?

339
00:21:59,110 --> 00:22:00,550
After a fashion.

340
00:22:03,290 --> 00:22:06,580
I prayed for my son Bran to survive his fall.

341
00:22:11,430 --> 00:22:12,820
Many years before that,

342
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:15,000
one of the boys came down with the pox.

343
00:22:16,880 --> 00:22:19,860
Maester Luwin said if he made it through the night, he'd live.

344
00:22:20,710 --> 00:22:22,950
But it would be a very long night.

345
00:22:26,010 --> 00:22:28,980
So I sat with him all through the darkness.

346
00:22:30,550 --> 00:22:32,550
Listened to his ragged little breaths,

347
00:22:33,970 --> 00:22:35,610
his coughing, his whimpering.

348
00:22:37,050 --> 00:22:37,910
Which boy?

349
00:22:39,790 --> 00:22:41,080
Jon Snow.

350
00:22:44,590 --> 00:22:46,750
When my husband brought that baby home from the war,

351
00:22:46,750 --> 00:22:48,620
I couldn't bear to look at him.

352
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:54,470
I didn't want to see those brown stranger's eyes staring up at me.

353
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:57,810
So I prayed to the gods, take him away.

354
00:22:57,990 --> 00:22:59,430
Make him die.

355
00:23:02,850 --> 00:23:04,050
He got the pox.

356
00:23:07,550 --> 00:23:11,280
And I knew I was the worst woman who ever lived.

357
00:23:13,550 --> 00:23:14,840
A murderer.

358
00:23:17,140 --> 00:23:20,980
I'd condemned this poor, innocent child to a horrible death

359
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:24,530
all because I was jealous of his mother.

360
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:27,160
A woman he didn't even know.

361
00:23:28,560 --> 00:23:30,870
So I prayed to all seven gods,

362
00:23:32,260 --> 00:23:33,360
let the boy live.

363
00:23:34,860 --> 00:23:35,940
Let him live

364
00:23:37,380 --> 00:23:38,410
and I'll love him.

365
00:23:39,570 --> 00:23:40,950
I'll be a mother to him.

366
00:23:42,650 --> 00:23:45,500
I'll beg my husband to give him a true name,

367
00:23:46,570 --> 00:23:48,860
to call him Stark and be done with it,

368
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:50,900
to make him one of us.

369
00:23:51,100 --> 00:23:52,020
And he lived.

370
00:23:53,550 --> 00:23:54,650
And he lived.

371
00:23:57,990 --> 00:24:00,090
And I couldn't keep my promise.

372
00:24:05,010 --> 00:24:06,910
And everything that's happened since then...

373
00:24:09,350 --> 00:24:11,850
all this horror that's come to my family...

374
00:24:14,910 --> 00:24:18,280
it's all because I couldn't love a motherless child.

375
00:24:29,270 --> 00:24:31,510
Was it hard for you to kill the Halfhand?

376
00:24:34,150 --> 00:24:34,870
Yes.

377
00:24:35,730 --> 00:24:36,580
You liked him?

378
00:24:38,450 --> 00:24:40,700
I like you, but if you're playing us false,

379
00:24:40,700 --> 00:24:42,340
it won't be hard for me to kill you.

380
00:24:42,590 --> 00:24:44,660
I've got wildling blood in my veins.

381
00:24:44,660 --> 00:24:47,600
- These are my people. - I understand.

382
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:49,700
Well, how could you understand?

383
00:24:50,880 --> 00:24:52,660
You want to protect your people.

384
00:24:53,860 --> 00:24:56,140
Do you know what it takes to unite 90 clans,

385
00:24:56,140 --> 00:24:59,830
half of whom want to massacre the other half for one insult or another?

386
00:25:00,870 --> 00:25:03,970
They speak seven different languages in my army.

387
00:25:04,690 --> 00:25:06,500
The Thenns hate the Hornfoots.

388
00:25:06,500 --> 00:25:08,710
The Hornfoots hate the ice-river clans.

389
00:25:09,040 --> 00:25:11,080
Everyone hates the cave people.

390
00:25:11,620 --> 00:25:16,560
So you know how I got moon worshippers and cannibals and giants

391
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:18,680
to march together in the same army?

392
00:25:19,550 --> 00:25:20,140
No.

393
00:25:20,850 --> 00:25:23,070
I told them we were all going to die

394
00:25:23,230 --> 00:25:24,860
if we don't get south.

395
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:27,160
Because that's the truth.

396
00:25:39,250 --> 00:25:40,530
Shouldn't be long now.

397
00:25:52,320 --> 00:25:53,400
What's wrong with him?

398
00:25:54,870 --> 00:25:55,960
He's a warg.

399
00:25:56,990 --> 00:25:59,730
He can enter the mind of animals, see through their eyes.

400
00:26:00,080 --> 00:26:01,380
He's scouting for us.

401
00:26:01,550 --> 00:26:03,480
What, you've never met a warg?

402
00:26:13,760 --> 00:26:14,430
Orell.

403
00:26:24,820 --> 00:26:26,430
Where were you this time?

404
00:26:32,550 --> 00:26:34,070
The Fist of the First Men.

405
00:26:34,970 --> 00:26:36,060
What did you see?

406
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:42,780
Dead crows.

407
00:26:53,570 --> 00:26:54,910
What's the matter, Piggy?

408
00:26:55,080 --> 00:26:56,380
You crying 'cause you're cold?

409
00:26:59,470 --> 00:27:02,580
There's 200 brothers killed by dead men

410
00:27:03,460 --> 00:27:04,440
and you're still here

411
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:06,700
whimpering.

412
00:27:08,650 --> 00:27:09,920
That seem fair to you?

413
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:13,810
I seen 'em torn to pieces fighting

414
00:27:13,810 --> 00:27:15,780
while you was off somewhere hiding in a hole.

415
00:27:17,710 --> 00:27:19,760
Why don't you lie down and rest for a while, eh?

416
00:27:21,990 --> 00:27:23,380
You know you want to.

417
00:27:48,550 --> 00:27:49,440
Get up, Sam.

418
00:27:50,070 --> 00:27:51,830
- No. - Get up.

419
00:27:52,690 --> 00:27:53,570
I can't.

420
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:56,210
If you stop, you'll die.

421
00:27:56,770 --> 00:28:00,440
'Course if you don't stop, you'll probably die, too.

422
00:28:00,440 --> 00:28:03,070
- You don't care. - 'Course we care.

423
00:28:03,460 --> 00:28:04,630
You left me.

424
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:08,940
When the white walkers came, you left me.

425
00:28:08,940 --> 00:28:11,090
Aye, we left you.

426
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:13,330
You're fat and you're slow.

427
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:15,280
We didn't want to die.

428
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:19,840
Help me get him up.

429
00:28:21,380 --> 00:28:22,980
Looks like that Piggy is done for.

430
00:28:22,980 --> 00:28:25,040
- Help us get him up. - He's slowing us down.

431
00:28:25,670 --> 00:28:26,920
Just get him up!

432
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:29,520
Why? So the rest of us can die?

433
00:28:31,740 --> 00:28:33,120
We'll move faster without him.

434
00:28:34,230 --> 00:28:37,100
What is this? Get up, Tarly.

435
00:28:39,230 --> 00:28:41,680
- Is he all right? - Aye, I think so.

436
00:28:41,680 --> 00:28:43,070
If he can't, we may as well just go without him.

437
00:28:43,070 --> 00:28:44,150
That's enough.

438
00:28:45,390 --> 00:28:46,570
Tarly,

439
00:28:47,350 --> 00:28:49,070
I forbid you to die.

440
00:28:50,120 --> 00:28:51,230
Do you hear me?

441
00:28:52,230 --> 00:28:53,830
- Why should the rest of us... - Rast...

442
00:28:54,510 --> 00:28:56,220
you're responsible for him.

443
00:28:57,470 --> 00:28:58,480
What am I supposed to do?

444
00:28:59,170 --> 00:29:00,800
Make sure he gets back alive.

445
00:29:01,420 --> 00:29:03,580
If he doesn't, you don't.

446
00:29:04,630 --> 00:29:05,520
Move on!

447
00:29:12,810 --> 00:29:14,280
I'm not dying for you, Piggy.

448
00:29:15,180 --> 00:29:16,290
You hear me?

449
00:29:42,170 --> 00:29:43,780
Where's Rickon and Hodor?

450
00:29:44,280 --> 00:29:45,430
Looking for food.

451
00:29:50,270 --> 00:29:51,380
What's out there?

452
00:30:41,510 --> 00:30:43,270
Not another step, boy.

453
00:30:43,790 --> 00:30:45,870
Unless you want to drown in your own blood.

454
00:30:45,870 --> 00:30:47,010
I'm unarmed.

455
00:30:47,540 --> 00:30:49,040
That was poor planning.

456
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:52,050
My sister carries the weapons.

457
00:30:54,270 --> 00:30:55,390
I'm better with them.

458
00:30:57,430 --> 00:30:58,460
Drop the spear.

459
00:31:01,870 --> 00:31:02,770
Drop it.

460
00:31:06,810 --> 00:31:07,800
If you kill me,

461
00:31:08,750 --> 00:31:10,590
that wolf will tear you to bits.

462
00:31:16,790 --> 00:31:17,820
You must be Summer.

463
00:31:42,260 --> 00:31:43,720
I'm Jojen Reed.

464
00:31:44,840 --> 00:31:47,170
This is my sister Meera.

465
00:31:52,220 --> 00:31:54,230
We've come a long way to find you, Brandon.

466
00:31:55,880 --> 00:31:57,600
And we have much farther to go.

467
00:32:02,140 --> 00:32:03,820
I'm just trying to understand.

468
00:32:04,500 --> 00:32:06,770
Would you please shut up about it?

469
00:32:06,770 --> 00:32:08,600
Jaqen H'ghar offered you three kills.

470
00:32:08,600 --> 00:32:10,860
- I'm not listening. - But just explain it to me.

471
00:32:11,330 --> 00:32:13,490
He offered to kill any three people you wanted.

472
00:32:13,490 --> 00:32:16,320
Dead. All you had to do was give him the names.

473
00:32:16,320 --> 00:32:18,920
Anyone. You could have picked King Joffrey.

474
00:32:18,920 --> 00:32:21,140
- Shut up. - You could have picked Tywin Lannister.

475
00:32:21,140 --> 00:32:23,910
Jaqen got us out of Harrenhal, so why are you complaining?

476
00:32:24,430 --> 00:32:25,940
But you could have ended the war.

477
00:32:27,010 --> 00:32:28,970
- Where are we going? - North.

478
00:32:29,320 --> 00:32:30,280
If we were going north,

479
00:32:30,280 --> 00:32:32,040
we should have come to the Red Fork River by now.

480
00:32:32,040 --> 00:32:33,640
Maybe we already passed it.

481
00:32:34,320 --> 00:32:35,780
It's 100 feet wide.

482
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:38,010
How could we have passed it?

483
00:32:39,100 --> 00:32:40,490
If we hit the Red Fork,

484
00:32:40,490 --> 00:32:42,150
we can follow it west to Riverrun.

485
00:32:42,150 --> 00:32:43,420
My mother grew up there.

486
00:32:43,590 --> 00:32:46,090
My grandfather's a lord. He'll protect us.

487
00:32:49,580 --> 00:32:51,100
- Could be a minstrel. - Shut up.

488
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:55,450
*A lion still has claws,*

489
00:32:55,610 --> 00:32:57,160
A minstrel's got gold sometimes.

490
00:32:57,830 --> 00:32:59,570
We could jump him, tie him up,

491
00:32:59,570 --> 00:33:01,610
steal his gold, and buy some food.

492
00:33:03,750 --> 00:33:07,030
*And so he spoke, and so he spoke*

493
00:33:07,030 --> 00:33:10,910
*that lord of Castamere,*

494
00:33:10,910 --> 00:33:15,160
*But now the rains weep o'er his hall,*

495
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:18,310
*with no one there to hear.*

496
00:33:22,180 --> 00:33:23,920
What's lurking behind that wall?

497
00:33:24,610 --> 00:33:26,680
A lion? A wolf?

498
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:30,060
Just a dirty little cub, I think.

499
00:33:30,060 --> 00:33:32,130
- Loose a few more shafts. - Don't.

500
00:33:44,550 --> 00:33:45,800
Put the sword down, girl.

501
00:33:47,520 --> 00:33:49,010
You go on down the road.

502
00:33:49,410 --> 00:33:51,420
Just keep on singing so we know where you are.

503
00:33:52,160 --> 00:33:54,580
Leave us be and I won't kill you.

504
00:33:58,330 --> 00:34:00,830
- Generous. - You're a dangerous person.

505
00:34:02,270 --> 00:34:03,670
I like dangerous people.

506
00:34:07,370 --> 00:34:09,410
Why are your friends so shy?

507
00:34:11,830 --> 00:34:12,840
What friends?

508
00:34:13,100 --> 00:34:15,610
The fat one to your left and the lad beside him.

509
00:34:37,220 --> 00:34:38,630
Three young ones on the run

510
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:40,980
carrying castle-forged swords.

511
00:34:42,120 --> 00:34:43,600
You escape from Harrenhal?

512
00:34:44,040 --> 00:34:46,330
- Who are you? - Thoros of Myr.

513
00:34:46,810 --> 00:34:48,630
And the fellow here with the bow is Anguy.

514
00:34:48,630 --> 00:34:50,050
No, who do you fight for?

515
00:34:50,050 --> 00:34:51,730
The Brotherhood without Banners.

516
00:34:54,430 --> 00:34:55,240
Now come along.

517
00:34:56,770 --> 00:34:59,890
I want to hear how two boys and a very dangerous girl

518
00:34:59,890 --> 00:35:01,800
escaped Harrenhal.

519
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:04,350
I'm not going with them. The Brotherhood?

520
00:35:04,830 --> 00:35:06,790
That's who the Mountain and them lot were looking for.

521
00:35:07,330 --> 00:35:08,840
They'll bring us back and put rats in us.

522
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:10,230
You've got nothing to fear from us, son.

523
00:35:11,160 --> 00:35:13,490
The lords of Westeros want to burn the countryside.

524
00:35:14,020 --> 00:35:15,540
We're trying to save it.

525
00:35:16,060 --> 00:35:16,670
Now come on.

526
00:35:17,330 --> 00:35:19,520
We'll talk some more over brown bread and stew.

527
00:35:20,280 --> 00:35:21,490
And then you can go on your way.

528
00:35:30,160 --> 00:35:31,750
Here's the thing, fat boy.

529
00:35:35,170 --> 00:35:36,520
When I'm done talking,

530
00:35:36,520 --> 00:35:39,520
that arrow's falling down on your fat head.

531
00:35:39,700 --> 00:35:43,200
So I advise you move, because I'm done talking.

532
00:35:58,240 --> 00:36:00,740
Half the country's starving and look at this one.

533
00:36:01,630 --> 00:36:03,710
Maybe he's the reason half the country's starving.

534
00:36:13,530 --> 00:36:14,420
My lion.

535
00:36:16,170 --> 00:36:17,570
We've spoken of this.

536
00:36:19,700 --> 00:36:22,200
You cannot come here.

537
00:36:25,400 --> 00:36:28,360
But you said I should come to you with any problems.

538
00:36:28,360 --> 00:36:29,450
Did anyone see you?

539
00:36:30,270 --> 00:36:31,500
- No. - You sure?

540
00:36:32,240 --> 00:36:34,280
My father does not make idle threats.

541
00:36:34,790 --> 00:36:37,200
Having you killed would be the high point of his week.

542
00:36:37,830 --> 00:36:39,510
You have to be absolutely certain.

543
00:36:43,920 --> 00:36:44,880
All right.

544
00:36:45,510 --> 00:36:46,950
What is this problem?

545
00:36:49,170 --> 00:36:51,330
I'm worried about Sansa.

546
00:36:51,470 --> 00:36:53,290
When she spoke with Lord Baelish,

547
00:36:53,290 --> 00:36:56,620
his friend warned me about him.

548
00:36:57,130 --> 00:36:58,790
Baelish doesn't have friends.

549
00:37:00,310 --> 00:37:01,560
I don't know her name.

550
00:37:01,780 --> 00:37:04,870
- Oh, Ros? The redhead? - How do you know her?

551
00:37:04,870 --> 00:37:06,770
I try to know as many people as I can.

552
00:37:06,770 --> 00:37:08,400
You never know which one you'll need.

553
00:37:09,520 --> 00:37:10,740
She's a whore.

554
00:37:10,740 --> 00:37:13,690
Yes, well, we shouldn't be judgmental about these things.

555
00:37:13,690 --> 00:37:16,190
- You fucked her. - Oh, please.

556
00:37:18,010 --> 00:37:20,090
All right, yes, I fucked her once.

557
00:37:22,640 --> 00:37:23,710
Twice.

558
00:37:24,970 --> 00:37:27,280
But since I met you, my lady,

559
00:37:27,870 --> 00:37:29,480
I've been with no one else.

560
00:37:32,540 --> 00:37:35,030
And was she good? Did you like her?

561
00:37:35,200 --> 00:37:36,990
Not as much as I like you, obviously,

562
00:37:36,990 --> 00:37:39,660
or she'd be the one torturing me right now.

563
00:37:43,230 --> 00:37:45,190
What did she say about Littlefinger?

564
00:37:48,350 --> 00:37:50,140
That Sansa shouldn't trust him.

565
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:54,020
Is there an idiot in any village who trusts Littlefinger?

566
00:37:57,490 --> 00:37:59,080
We have to protect her.

567
00:37:59,290 --> 00:38:00,040
We can't.

568
00:38:00,960 --> 00:38:03,020
Now that the Lannisters have discarded her,

569
00:38:03,660 --> 00:38:05,530
Sansa will have many suitors.

570
00:38:05,940 --> 00:38:08,620
A great beauty with a very old name.

571
00:38:09,240 --> 00:38:12,120
A great beauty? Oh, a great beauty?

572
00:38:12,120 --> 00:38:15,060
Yes, she is, objectively, very.

573
00:38:15,500 --> 00:38:17,760
Her face is quite pleasing--

574
00:38:17,760 --> 00:38:19,720
to other men and to women,

575
00:38:19,720 --> 00:38:21,200
people in general.

576
00:38:21,690 --> 00:38:23,060
But not to me, of course.

577
00:38:24,280 --> 00:38:26,070
I only have eyes for you.

578
00:38:26,870 --> 00:38:29,290
You pervert. You want her?

579
00:38:29,290 --> 00:38:32,330
- This child? - I don't even want to talk about her.

580
00:38:32,780 --> 00:38:35,430
- But you are. - Only because you're making me.

581
00:38:36,500 --> 00:38:40,440
I didn't force you to open your mouth and make words come out.

582
00:38:40,440 --> 00:38:43,620
This is cruel and unfair. Cruelly unfair.

583
00:38:44,290 --> 00:38:48,020
"Oh, the cruelty. Shae is so cruel to me."

584
00:38:50,510 --> 00:38:51,830
Hmm, yes.

585
00:39:01,670 --> 00:39:03,310
Now do you think I'm cruel?

586
00:39:04,910 --> 00:39:06,240
Only if you stop.

587
00:39:08,760 --> 00:39:10,400
But you mustn't come here again.

588
00:39:14,380 --> 00:39:15,380
Do you hear me?

589
00:39:18,330 --> 00:39:20,390
You've come to a dangerous place

590
00:39:21,320 --> 00:39:22,910
full of dangerous people.

591
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:27,220
Dangerous people.

592
00:39:38,200 --> 00:39:39,460
Lady Tyrell, my king.

593
00:39:39,460 --> 00:39:40,250
Send her in.

594
00:39:56,320 --> 00:39:58,120
You wanted to see me, Your Grace?

595
00:39:58,480 --> 00:39:59,950
I'm leaving on a hunting trip.

596
00:40:00,140 --> 00:40:02,570
I just wanted to make sure

597
00:40:02,570 --> 00:40:04,560
you had everything you need before I left.

598
00:40:04,560 --> 00:40:06,620
That's very thoughtful, Your Grace.

599
00:40:06,860 --> 00:40:08,450
I have everything I could want.

600
00:40:08,450 --> 00:40:09,960
Good. Good.

601
00:40:10,360 --> 00:40:12,040
How are you finding life in the capital?

602
00:40:12,750 --> 00:40:15,410
It must be quite a change after Renly's camp.

603
00:40:15,410 --> 00:40:16,510
A welcome one.

604
00:40:17,200 --> 00:40:21,130
A military encampment is no place for a lady.

605
00:40:21,890 --> 00:40:23,600
And the bedside of a traitor?

606
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:29,000
Is that a place for a lady?

607
00:40:29,790 --> 00:40:30,660
Your Grace,

608
00:40:32,960 --> 00:40:36,080
I tried to do my duty as a wife, that is all.

609
00:40:36,260 --> 00:40:39,380
What was your duty to this traitor as you saw it?

610
00:40:39,770 --> 00:40:41,750
The duty of any wife to any husband--

611
00:40:41,940 --> 00:40:43,540
to provide him with children.

612
00:40:43,700 --> 00:40:45,050
You failed to do this.

613
00:40:46,280 --> 00:40:47,450
- Why? - I--

614
00:40:51,170 --> 00:40:53,720
I would not speak ill of the dead, Your Grace.

615
00:40:53,720 --> 00:40:57,180
You think one ought to speak kindly of a traitor merely because

616
00:40:57,180 --> 00:40:58,660
he's had a sword put through his heart?

617
00:40:58,660 --> 00:40:59,210
No.

618
00:41:00,400 --> 00:41:02,480
I do beg your pardon.

619
00:41:03,530 --> 00:41:06,480
The subtleties of politics are often lost on me.

620
00:41:07,030 --> 00:41:07,840
Renly...

621
00:41:11,270 --> 00:41:13,690
I don't believe he was interested

622
00:41:13,690 --> 00:41:15,540
in the company of women.

623
00:41:17,810 --> 00:41:19,030
What makes you say this?

624
00:41:21,480 --> 00:41:24,800
Whenever I wanted to make a child with him,

625
00:41:24,800 --> 00:41:25,340
he--

626
00:41:27,930 --> 00:41:29,340
he had so many excuses,

627
00:41:29,340 --> 00:41:31,370
so many late-night war councils.

628
00:41:32,050 --> 00:41:33,570
He never wanted to try.

629
00:41:35,420 --> 00:41:37,830
Except one evening,

630
00:41:38,380 --> 00:41:40,870
after he'd had far too much wine to drink,

631
00:41:40,870 --> 00:41:42,850
he suggested something--

632
00:41:46,970 --> 00:41:50,280
something that sounded very painful

633
00:41:50,280 --> 00:41:52,700
and couldn't possibly result in children.

634
00:41:54,800 --> 00:41:56,310
Maybe the fault was with me.

635
00:41:56,310 --> 00:41:58,490
No, he was a known...

636
00:42:00,200 --> 00:42:01,250
degenerate.

637
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:04,640
It's such a relief to hear you say so, Your Grace.

638
00:42:06,110 --> 00:42:08,760
I've considered making his perversion

639
00:42:08,760 --> 00:42:10,230
punishable by death.

640
00:42:12,430 --> 00:42:13,790
As is your right.

641
00:42:15,640 --> 00:42:18,640
You must do whatever you need to do.

642
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:23,980
You are the king.

643
00:42:25,150 --> 00:42:25,720
Yes.

644
00:42:29,580 --> 00:42:30,520
I am.

645
00:42:37,190 --> 00:42:38,310
Do you like it?

646
00:42:39,460 --> 00:42:40,380
I just had it made.

647
00:42:40,380 --> 00:42:43,300
Probably one of the finest weapons in the Seven Kingdoms.

648
00:42:43,300 --> 00:42:44,550
It's beautiful.

649
00:42:44,870 --> 00:42:46,360
Will you show me how it works?

650
00:42:51,230 --> 00:42:52,270
It's a new design.

651
00:42:52,680 --> 00:42:54,800
Much easier to load. There's no crank.

652
00:42:55,280 --> 00:42:59,000
You use this lever to draw the string.

653
00:43:04,300 --> 00:43:06,000
The bolt goes here.

654
00:43:09,010 --> 00:43:12,680
And then you just aim...

655
00:43:15,410 --> 00:43:16,460
and...

656
00:43:21,820 --> 00:43:23,800
Would you take me hunting sometime?

657
00:43:25,330 --> 00:43:26,270
Forgive me, Your Grace.

658
00:43:26,270 --> 00:43:28,750
I know a hunt is no place for a woman.

659
00:43:28,750 --> 00:43:29,930
It's not unheard of.

660
00:43:30,340 --> 00:43:32,650
My father would never let me before--

661
00:43:32,860 --> 00:43:34,790
You no longer belong to him.

662
00:43:36,410 --> 00:43:37,530
Do you want to hold it?

663
00:43:38,220 --> 00:43:40,310
May I? Please.

664
00:43:48,140 --> 00:43:50,210
I imagine it must be so exciting

665
00:43:50,210 --> 00:43:51,590
to squeeze your finger here

666
00:43:51,590 --> 00:43:55,090
and watch something die over there.

667
00:43:59,090 --> 00:44:00,380
Could you do it?

668
00:44:01,850 --> 00:44:02,660
Could you...

669
00:44:03,580 --> 00:44:05,470
kill something?

670
00:44:07,160 --> 00:44:09,280
I don't know, Your Grace. Do you think I could?

671
00:44:09,800 --> 00:44:10,630
Yes.

672
00:44:12,330 --> 00:44:13,680
Would you like to watch me?

673
00:44:18,510 --> 00:44:19,590
Yes.

674
00:44:23,190 --> 00:44:24,560
Stop! Stop!

675
00:44:24,560 --> 00:44:27,150
- Tell us the truth. - About what?

676
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:30,760
I don't know what you want.

677
00:44:30,760 --> 00:44:33,070
I want the truth.

678
00:44:33,070 --> 00:44:34,510
What truth?

679
00:44:34,860 --> 00:44:36,230
Why'd you take Winterfell?

680
00:44:37,200 --> 00:44:39,240
I took it to-- I took it--

681
00:44:39,240 --> 00:44:40,760
- Who gave the orders? - No one.

682
00:44:40,950 --> 00:44:42,470
- I took it on my own. - Why?

683
00:44:42,470 --> 00:44:44,360
To take the North while it was vulnerable.

684
00:44:44,540 --> 00:44:46,020
What were you going to do once you took it?

685
00:44:46,020 --> 00:44:47,710
Hold it. Rule it.

686
00:44:47,710 --> 00:44:50,670
Good. That's very good.

687
00:44:56,330 --> 00:44:57,890
Why did you do that? I told you.

688
00:44:57,890 --> 00:44:59,670
Why did you take Winterfell?

689
00:44:59,670 --> 00:45:03,530
I took it to bring glory to my house and my father.

690
00:45:11,230 --> 00:45:12,920
Wait. Wait, wait, wait.

691
00:45:12,920 --> 00:45:16,470
I took it because I hated the Starks.

692
00:45:16,470 --> 00:45:19,040
I hated them for holding me prisoner.

693
00:45:19,040 --> 00:45:20,560
I wanted to hurt them.

694
00:45:27,430 --> 00:45:28,510
What are you doing?

695
00:45:32,820 --> 00:45:33,630
Please.

696
00:45:39,740 --> 00:45:41,280
I'll tell you anything.

697
00:45:42,780 --> 00:45:44,250
Just take it off.

698
00:45:44,690 --> 00:45:47,570
Please. Please, just take it off.

699
00:45:57,310 --> 00:45:59,470
- Your sister sent me. - Yara?

700
00:46:07,330 --> 00:46:09,560
I'll come for you tonight when the castle sleeps.

701
00:46:09,560 --> 00:46:11,560
No, don't go. Please, please.

702
00:46:11,560 --> 00:46:13,080
No, please don't leave me.

703
00:46:13,850 --> 00:46:14,880
Please, please.

704
00:46:15,460 --> 00:46:16,580
Please, please don't go!

705
00:46:16,580 --> 00:46:19,780
Please. Please. Don't leave me.

706
00:46:21,720 --> 00:46:23,060
Please don't go.

707
00:46:28,860 --> 00:46:30,120
Rickon, stay with us.

708
00:46:32,060 --> 00:46:35,450
It's all right. The wolves will protect him.

709
00:46:45,270 --> 00:46:46,710
You can get inside his head,

710
00:46:47,340 --> 00:46:48,540
see through his eyes.

711
00:46:49,260 --> 00:46:50,320
Only when I'm asleep.

712
00:46:50,610 --> 00:46:52,360
That's how it begins until you learn to control it.

713
00:46:53,280 --> 00:46:54,300
You're a warg.

714
00:46:54,930 --> 00:46:56,330
It's not just wolves.

715
00:46:57,180 --> 00:46:58,820
Sometimes in my dreams there's a--

716
00:46:58,820 --> 00:47:01,130
- A three-eyed raven? - You've seen it?

717
00:47:01,770 --> 00:47:03,810
We saw him together. You haven't forgotten?

718
00:47:04,730 --> 00:47:06,490
Does it have anything to do with warging?

719
00:47:06,830 --> 00:47:09,150
No, the raven is something different,

720
00:47:09,340 --> 00:47:10,380
something deeper.

721
00:47:12,280 --> 00:47:13,570
The raven brings the sight.

722
00:47:14,090 --> 00:47:16,310
Seeing things that haven't happened yet?

723
00:47:16,620 --> 00:47:18,420
Or things that happened long before you were born

724
00:47:18,420 --> 00:47:21,910
or things that are happening right now thousands of miles away.

725
00:47:23,160 --> 00:47:24,140
What's he telling him?

726
00:47:24,950 --> 00:47:26,100
Why don't you ask?

727
00:47:28,930 --> 00:47:30,260
Isn't he ashamed, your brother,

728
00:47:30,780 --> 00:47:32,210
needing you to protect him?

729
00:47:33,210 --> 00:47:34,490
Where's the shame in that?

730
00:47:35,310 --> 00:47:38,400
Any boy his age who needs his sister to protect him

731
00:47:38,870 --> 00:47:41,530
is gonna find himself needing lots of protecting.

732
00:47:43,620 --> 00:47:45,350
Some people will always need help.

733
00:47:47,270 --> 00:47:48,880
That doesn't mean they're not worth helping.

734
00:47:53,340 --> 00:47:56,130
When my father died, I dreamt it.

735
00:47:58,030 --> 00:47:59,650
You didn't dream it. You saw it.

736
00:48:00,630 --> 00:48:01,470
So did I.

737
00:48:02,520 --> 00:48:03,880
You have the sight, too?

738
00:48:07,030 --> 00:48:10,760
When I told my father about your father...

739
00:48:12,210 --> 00:48:14,310
for the first time in my life, I saw him cry.

740
00:48:15,400 --> 00:48:16,830
Your father is Howland Reed?

741
00:48:17,540 --> 00:48:18,070
Yeah.

742
00:48:18,940 --> 00:48:22,110
He saved my father's life during the rebellion.

743
00:48:22,110 --> 00:48:23,670
Your father told you about the rebellion?

744
00:48:24,490 --> 00:48:25,490
Mine never did.

745
00:48:26,340 --> 00:48:27,540
But I saw that, too.

746
00:48:28,830 --> 00:48:30,020
What else have you seen?

747
00:48:31,700 --> 00:48:32,970
Only one thing that matters--

748
00:48:34,200 --> 00:48:34,760
you.

749
00:48:46,320 --> 00:48:47,800
I never liked the skinny ones.

750
00:48:48,470 --> 00:48:49,900
Like drinking from a puddle.

751
00:48:50,360 --> 00:48:52,230
Not that I'm averse to drinking from a puddle

752
00:48:52,230 --> 00:48:53,560
every now and again, you understand.

753
00:48:53,560 --> 00:48:54,480
I don't drink ale.

754
00:48:55,190 --> 00:48:58,080
There's no story so good a drink won't make it better.

755
00:48:58,250 --> 00:48:59,610
- Yeah! - You see?

756
00:49:00,050 --> 00:49:01,920
They've all suffered through my bouts of sobriety.

757
00:49:01,920 --> 00:49:03,770
It's very tedious for all concerned.

758
00:49:04,460 --> 00:49:06,860
Now, how did three children--

759
00:49:06,860 --> 00:49:07,750
We're not children.

760
00:49:08,710 --> 00:49:11,090
How did three young persons such as yourselves,

761
00:49:11,090 --> 00:49:13,210
untrained in the art of war,

762
00:49:14,230 --> 00:49:15,520
escape from Harrenhal?

763
00:49:18,180 --> 00:49:19,210
Gendry's a smith.

764
00:49:20,230 --> 00:49:21,510
He was apprenticed in the armory.

765
00:49:21,660 --> 00:49:24,250
A smith, eh? Where'd you train?

766
00:49:28,170 --> 00:49:30,430
King's Landing. Tobho Mott's shop.

767
00:49:30,600 --> 00:49:32,950
That criminal. He charges twice as much

768
00:49:32,950 --> 00:49:34,530
as every other armorer in the city.

769
00:49:34,530 --> 00:49:35,860
That's because he's twice as good.

770
00:49:35,860 --> 00:49:39,210
Aha! A smith and a salesman.

771
00:49:39,880 --> 00:49:41,060
Gendry stole us weapons.

772
00:49:41,060 --> 00:49:44,590
Ah, fought your way out of Harrenhal, I see.

773
00:49:45,500 --> 00:49:48,250
He knows how to use a sword and so do I.

774
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:51,490
My brothers taught me.

775
00:50:12,910 --> 00:50:14,170
To your brothers!

776
00:50:23,040 --> 00:50:24,590
You can finish your meals before you go.

777
00:50:25,400 --> 00:50:27,080
It may be awhile before you see another.

778
00:50:27,710 --> 00:50:30,030
- You'll free us? - I gave you my word.

779
00:50:33,470 --> 00:50:34,580
But before you go,

780
00:50:35,590 --> 00:50:38,000
allow me to raise a cup to your--

781
00:50:41,530 --> 00:50:43,790
Now that is an uncommonly large person.

782
00:50:43,790 --> 00:50:47,210
How does one manage to subdue such an uncommonly large person?

783
00:50:47,210 --> 00:50:49,990
One waits for him to drink until he passes out.

784
00:50:49,990 --> 00:50:52,380
Poor man. You have my sympathy.

785
00:50:54,600 --> 00:50:57,650
Aha, not a man at all.

786
00:50:59,050 --> 00:51:00,670
A Hound!

787
00:51:02,010 --> 00:51:04,040
So good to see you again, Clegane.

788
00:51:04,680 --> 00:51:05,660
Thoros?

789
00:51:07,120 --> 00:51:08,930
The fuck you doing here?

790
00:51:09,660 --> 00:51:11,940
Drinking and talking too much. Same as ever.

791
00:51:13,110 --> 00:51:16,070
- A pretty prize, lads. - Yeah!

792
00:51:18,970 --> 00:51:19,920
Girl.

793
00:51:22,400 --> 00:51:23,800
What in seven hells

794
00:51:24,090 --> 00:51:26,260
are you doing with the Stark bitch?

795
00:51:37,800 --> 00:51:39,310
It's a tough decision--

796
00:51:39,810 --> 00:51:41,860
take the bridge and risk being seen

797
00:51:42,460 --> 00:51:43,980
or cross the great water.

798
00:51:43,980 --> 00:51:46,450
- Silence, Kingslayer. - Anyone can see us on the bridge,

799
00:51:46,760 --> 00:51:49,730
but cross by water and the current could take us

800
00:51:49,730 --> 00:51:51,490
or I could escape down the river.

801
00:51:51,490 --> 00:51:52,530
Good luck.

802
00:51:55,730 --> 00:51:59,050
It's wonderful to watch you wrestle with these dilemmas.

803
00:51:59,640 --> 00:52:01,410
Which will she choose?

804
00:52:06,770 --> 00:52:08,360
Gambler at heart.

805
00:52:08,560 --> 00:52:09,800
Wouldn't have guessed.

806
00:52:10,130 --> 00:52:11,430
Be quick about it.

807
00:52:19,100 --> 00:52:20,660
- I need to rest. - Get up.

808
00:52:20,660 --> 00:52:23,240
I have these, you know, on your feet when you walk too far.

809
00:52:23,240 --> 00:52:24,670
- What do you call them? - Get up now.

810
00:52:24,670 --> 00:52:27,330
Corns. I never used to get corns.

811
00:52:27,860 --> 00:52:29,420
Of course I used to ride everywhere,

812
00:52:29,620 --> 00:52:31,780
not march around like a common foot soldier

813
00:52:31,780 --> 00:52:34,260
wearing the same shit boots for over a year.

814
00:52:34,260 --> 00:52:36,740
This heel is ruined. There's no way--

815
00:52:44,690 --> 00:52:48,020
I never understood why some knights felt the need to carry two swords.

816
00:53:09,020 --> 00:53:10,210
You move well...

817
00:53:14,080 --> 00:53:15,700
for a great beast of a woman.

818
00:53:20,300 --> 00:53:22,220
You shouldn't grimace before you lunge.

819
00:53:22,220 --> 00:53:23,340
It gives away the game.

820
00:53:38,560 --> 00:53:39,840
Bit of a quandary for you.

821
00:53:40,260 --> 00:53:42,970
If you kill me, you fail Lady Stark.

822
00:53:43,760 --> 00:53:45,150
But if you don't kill me,

823
00:53:46,770 --> 00:53:48,250
I'm going to kill you.

824
00:53:54,190 --> 00:53:55,210
You're good.

825
00:53:56,110 --> 00:53:57,980
Graceless, but good.

826
00:54:07,440 --> 00:54:09,740
See? If you were willing to hurt me,

827
00:54:09,740 --> 00:54:11,040
you might have had me there.

828
00:54:54,830 --> 00:54:57,230
Looks like your woman's getting the better of you,

829
00:54:57,990 --> 00:54:59,610
if you can call that a woman.

830
00:54:59,920 --> 00:55:01,350
We enjoy a good fight.

831
00:55:01,790 --> 00:55:03,270
Gets our juices flowing.

832
00:55:04,430 --> 00:55:06,660
The Flayed Man of House Bolton.

833
00:55:07,410 --> 00:55:08,800
A bit gruesome for my taste.

834
00:55:08,800 --> 00:55:12,650
- You sure he's the one? - That's him, all right.

835
00:55:13,090 --> 00:55:16,150
I saw him fight at the tourney for Ser Willem Frey's wedding.

836
00:55:18,250 --> 00:55:19,710
Give the man his silver.

837
00:55:21,910 --> 00:55:24,940
Let us go and my father will pay you whatever you want.

838
00:55:25,100 --> 00:55:27,030
Enough to buy me a new head?

839
00:55:27,760 --> 00:55:30,230
If the King in the North hears I had the Kingslayer

840
00:55:30,230 --> 00:55:32,520
and let him go, he'll be taking it right off.

841
00:55:33,410 --> 00:55:35,050
I'd rather he takes yours.

